{"items":[{"recordId":"teach-n0001106","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"In ΔL, every proper net is canonical.","requires":["definition of proper net"],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"For any n beta-reductions, there are exactly n corresponding interactions in the net, and those interactions may occur in any order.","requires":["definition of beta-reduction","definition of net interaction"],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"A beta-reduction is one computational step that replaces an application of a function to an argument with the function body where the argument is substituted.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false},{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"Canonical means satisfying the designated canonical-form property of the formal system; this statement does not by itself specify how canonicality is established.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false}]},{"recordId":"teach-n0001107","units":[{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"ΔA requires erasure canonicalization, while ΔI requires replicator merging.","requires":["system-specific meaning of erasure canonicalization","system-specific meaning of replicator merging"],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"statement","basis":"source","text":"ΔK requires erasure canonicalization, merge canonicalization, and decay canonicalization.","requires":["system-specific meaning of erasure canonicalization","system-specific meaning of merge canonicalization","system-specific meaning of decay canonicalization"],"citationRequired":true},{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"Canonicalization is the process of transforming a representation so that it satisfies the formal system’s chosen canonical property.","requires":[],"citationRequired":false},{"role":"definition","basis":"common_knowledge","text":"Erasure, merging, and decay name distinct net operations or behaviors here, but their precise meanings are specific to the ΔA, ΔI, and ΔK systems.","requires":["definitions of erasure, merging, and decay in these systems"],"citationRequired":false}]}]}
